<!-- The hostile shape the "no raw HTML" claim used to deny: an Atom entry that
     declares its content and summary as `type="text"` while the escaped text
     spells out HTML. Escaped `&lt;script&gt;` is XML transport encoding, so the
     text feed-rs hands over is literally `<script>alert(1)</script>`; a
     text-typed value is not run through the HTML→Markdown converter at all, so
     it is stored byte-exact, tag-shaped content included.

     This is the documented passthrough, not a conversion bug — declaring
     `type="text"` is the feed asserting the value is not markup, and Skardi
     stores what it was told. The fixture exists so that the behaviour is a
     reviewed contract: `plaintext_typed_content_is_stored_byte_exact` pins the
     exact stored strings, so a converter change that starts rewriting
     text-typed values fails a test, and the docs' narrower claim ("no HTML tag
     survives as markup") stays honest. -->
